## Question: what software generally we have in a pos machine?

Devmate
Full
A POS (Point of Sale) machine runs a layered software stack. Here's what's generally on one:

1. Operating System
Embedded/mobile OS — commonly Android (most modern terminals), Linux, or a proprietary RTOS. Older terminals used Windows CE/Windows Embedded.
Often a hardened/locked-down variant for security certification.
2. POS Application (the "till" software)
The merchant-facing app that runs the sale:

Item catalog, pricing, discounts, tax calculation
Cart/transaction management
Receipt generation (print/email/SMS)
Inventory and sales reporting
User/cashier login and shift management
3. Payment Application (EMV kernel + apps)
This is the regulated, certified part:

EMV kernel(s) — contact (chip) and contactless (NFC) transaction processing logic, certified to EMVCo specs.
Card brand applications — Visa, Mastercard, Amex, etc. payment apps.
PIN entry / PIN pad software (PED firmware) — handles secure PIN capture.
Tokenization / encryption modules — P2PE (point-to-point encryption).
4. Payment Gateway / Processor Client
Software that talks to the acquirer/payment processor (authorization, settlement, batch close).
Handles communication protocols (ISO 8583 messaging, or modern REST/TLS APIs).
5. Security Layer
PCI-DSS / PCI-PTS compliance components
Secure element / HSM (Hardware Security Module) firmware for key storage
Cryptographic libraries (encryption, key management, certificates)
Tamper detection firmware
6. Peripheral Drivers
Software to drive attached hardware:

Card readers (magstripe, chip, NFC)
Receipt printer
Barcode/QR scanner
Customer-facing display
Cash drawer
7. Connectivity & Device Management
Network stack — Wi-Fi, Ethernet, cellular (4G/LTE)
Terminal Management System (TMS) client — remote config, software/firmware updates (OTA), monitoring, diagnostics
Logging and telemetry
8. Optional Business Integrations
Loyalty/rewards programs
Gift card handling
CRM / accounting sync (e.g., to a backend ERP)
Offline mode (store-and-forward transactions when network drops)
Architecturally, the key split is between the business/POS app (flexible, merchant-customizable) and the secure payment stack (locked-down, certified, isolated — often running in a separate secure processor or trusted execution environment). This separation is what allows the payment portion to stay PCI/EMVCo certified while the POS app can be updated freely.


