## Question: so if i am given a task to create app for pos device for payment processing. what architecture should I use?

Devmate
Full
The single most important architectural principle for a POS payment app: isolate the certified payment logic from your business app. Everything else follows from that.

Here's the architecture I'd recommend:

Core principle: Two-zone separation
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  NORMAL ZONE (your app — freely updatable)   │
│  • POS/business UI                            │
│  • Cart, catalog, receipts                    │
│  • Backend/cloud sync                         │
└───────────────────┬─────────────────────────┘
                     │  thin, well-defined API
┌───────────────────▼─────────────────────────┐
│  SECURE ZONE (certified — touch as little    │
│  as possible)                                 │
│  • EMV kernel (contact + contactless)         │
│  • PIN entry (PED)                            │
│  • Encryption / tokenization / key mgmt       │
│  • Secure element / TEE                        │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
Card data and PINs should never flow through your business app. Your app says "charge $20"; the secure zone returns "approved, token=xyz." This keeps your app out of PCI-DSS scope (or drastically reduces it), which is a massive cost/effort saver.

Recommended layering
Layer	Responsibility	Notes
Presentation	Cashier/customer UI	Keep dumb — no payment logic here
Business logic	Cart, pricing, tax, inventory	Your domain
Payment orchestration	State machine for a transaction	The critical layer — see below
Payment SDK / abstraction	Wraps the terminal vendor's SDK	Hide vendor specifics behind your own interface
Secure/EMV stack	Vendor-provided, certified	You usually don't write this — you call it
Connectivity	Gateway/processor comms, retries, offline queue	
Key architectural decisions
1. Use the terminal vendor's payment SDK — don't build the EMV kernel. On Android terminals (Verifone, Ingenico/PAX, Stripe Terminal, Square, Adyen, etc.) you get a certified SDK. Writing/certifying your own EMV kernel is a multi-year, hugely expensive effort. Wrap their SDK behind your own interface so you can swap vendors later.

2. Model the transaction as an explicit state machine. Payment flows are full of edge cases (card removed mid-read, network drop during auth, partial approval, reversal needed). A clear state machine is the difference between a robust and a buggy POS app:

IDLE → AMOUNT_ENTERED → CARD_READ → ONLINE_AUTH
   → APPROVED / DECLINED → RECEIPT → IDLE
   (+ error/reversal/timeout transitions from each state)
3. Plan for offline / store-and-forward from day one. Networks drop. Decide upfront: queue transactions locally (encrypted) and forward when back online, vs. hard-decline. This is hard to retrofit.

4. Idempotency everywhere. Every transaction needs a unique ID so retries after a timeout don't double-charge. This must be end-to-end (app → gateway → processor).

5. Async, event-driven communication with the secure zone. The EMV flow is inherently async (waiting on card, PIN, network). Use callbacks/events, not blocking calls.

What I'd pick concretely (typical modern stack)
Platform: Android-based smart terminal (dominant today; rich SDKs, app-store deployment).
App language: Kotlin, clean architecture (presentation / domain / data).
Payment abstraction: Your own PaymentProcessor interface → adapter → vendor SDK.
Local store: Encrypted DB (SQLCipher/Room) for offline queue + receipts.
Backend comms: TLS, idempotency keys, exponential-backoff retries.
Updates: OTA via the vendor's TMS, with the secure zone updated separately.
