Great interview question — the key insight to land is that payment authorization and actual money movement are two separate phases. Authorization happens in ~2 seconds; the money actually moves hours/days later. Let me walk through both.

The players (the "four-party model")
Entity	Role
Cardholder	The customer paying
Merchant	The business getting paid
Acquirer (merchant's bank)	Bank that holds the merchant's account, processes their transactions
Issuer (customer's bank)	Bank that issued the customer's card
Card network	Visa/Mastercard/RuPay — the "switch" routing between acquirer & issuer
Payment processor / gateway	Tech middlemen that move the messages
Phase 1: Authorization (the ~2-second real-time flow)
This checks if funds exist and reserves them — no money moves yet.

1. Customer taps/inserts card at terminal
2. Terminal + EMV kernel read card, build an encrypted auth request
        (card data → tokenized/encrypted)
3. Terminal → Acquirer/Processor (auth request)
4. Acquirer → Card Network (Visa/MC/RuPay) — routes by card number (BIN)
5. Network → Issuer (customer's bank)
6. Issuer checks:


## Question: • Is the card valid / not expired / not blocked?




## Question: • Are funds / credit available?

• Fraud & risk scoring (location, amount, velocity)
        • PIN / CVV / cryptogram verification
7. Issuer → APPROVE or DECLINE → back along the SAME path
        Issuer → Network → Acquirer → Terminal
8. Terminal shows "Approved", prints receipt
        → Issuer places a HOLD on the customer's funds (not yet debited)
Result of Phase 1: funds are reserved, merchant has an approval code. Customer walks away. But the merchant doesn't have the money yet.

Phase 2: Clearing & Settlement (the actual money movement, hours–days later)
9.  End of day: merchant "batches" all approved transactions
        → sends batch to Acquirer
10. Acquirer → Card Network (clearing): submits transactions
11. Network calculates who owes whom, and the fees
12. Issuer debits the customer's account for real
13. Issuer → Network → Acquirer: funds transferred
        (minus interchange + network + acquirer fees)
14. Acquirer credits the MERCHANT's account (T+1 / T+2 typically)
So the merchant typically gets paid 1–2 business days later, net of fees.

Who takes a cut (the fee chain)
Customer pays ₹1000 → merchant might receive ~₹980:

Interchange fee → goes to the issuer (biggest slice)
Network/scheme fee → Visa/Mastercard/RuPay
Acquirer markup → merchant's bank/processor
This bundled fee is the Merchant Discount Rate (MDR).

The authorization decision in detail (step 6 — what interviewers probe)
The issuer decides approve/decline based on:

Card validity — active, not expired, not reported lost/stolen
Sufficient funds / credit limit
Cryptogram verification — the chip generated a unique cryptographic code (ARQC); issuer verifies it → proves card is genuine, not cloned
PIN/CVV check — cardholder verification
Fraud/risk engine — ML scoring on amount, merchant category, geography, spending velocity, unusual patterns


## Question: Velocity / limit rules — too many txns too fast?

Key concepts to name-drop in the interview
Authorization ≠ Settlement — the core distinction. Auth reserves funds; settlement moves them.
Hold / Auth hold — funds reserved but not captured.
Capture — merchant confirms the sale (sometimes separate from auth, e.g., hotels/fuel).
ARQC (cryptogram) — the chip's proof of authenticity, verified by the issuer.
ISO 8583 — the legacy messaging standard for these auth/clearing messages (modern systems moving toward ISO 20022).
BIN — first 6–8 digits of card; tells the network which issuer to route to.
T+1 / T+2 — settlement timing to the merchant.
Quick contrast: UPI (India) — much simpler, real-time
Customer scans QR → enters UPI PIN
→ PSP app → NPCI switch → debits customer's bank
→ NPCI → credits merchant's bank — INSTANTLY (real-time)
UPI collapses auth and settlement into near-instant account-to-account transfer (no card networks, no interchange, near-zero MDR). That's why it's a great compare/contrast point.

One-line summary for the interview:

"Money flow has two phases: authorization, a ~2-second round trip where the terminal asks the issuer — via acquirer and card network — to verify the card and reserve funds; and settlement, which happens hours later when batches are cleared and the issuer actually transfers money to the merchant's acquirer, minus interchange and network fees. The chip's cryptogram and the issuer's fraud engine are what make the auth decision trustworthy."


