## Question: why UPI is not used globally?

Devmate
Full
Good question — and the nuance worth landing is that UPI's limits are mostly political, economic, and structural — not technical. The tech works fine; it's everything around it that doesn't transfer easily. Here's the breakdown:

1. It's built for India's specific infrastructure
UPI runs on top of the IMPS real-time bank rails and is operated by NPCI — a single, government-backed national entity.
Most countries either don't have an equivalent always-on real-time bank-transfer rail, or have several fragmented ones.
UPI assumes a unified national system; most markets are far more fragmented.
2. No single equivalent of NPCI elsewhere
UPI works because one not-for-profit body (backed by RBI) coordinates every bank.
In the US/EU, payments are run by competing private companies (Visa, Mastercard, banks, fintechs) with no incentive to hand control to a shared free utility.
Building UPI elsewhere needs a central coordinating authority that most countries lack.
3. The business-model problem (this is the big one)
UPI is near-zero cost (zero MDR) for merchants — great for adoption, but nobody makes money moving the transaction.
The global card ecosystem runs on interchange fees (~1–3%). Visa, Mastercard, and banks earn billions from this.
They have no incentive to replace a profitable system with a free one. UPI threatens their revenue, so there's active resistance.
4. Regulatory & data sovereignty barriers
Cross-border money movement triggers AML, KYC, sanctions, FX controls, local licensing — all country-specific.
Data localization laws, banking regulations, and central-bank approval differ everywhere.
A system that's "one country, one regulator" doesn't port cleanly across borders.
5. India had unique enabling conditions
Aadhaar (national digital ID) → instant, cheap bank-account verification and KYC.
Jan Dhan → hundreds of millions of new bank accounts in a short window.
Cheap mobile data (Jio) → smartphones everywhere.
Government push (demonetization 2016) → forced digital adoption.
This "India Stack" combination is rare; few countries have all the pieces.
6. Trust, habit, and incumbency
The West already had deeply entrenched, convenient systems — cards, Apple/Google Pay, PayPal. No painful gap for UPI to fill.
Consumer habits and merchant infrastructure are already built around cards.
But — UPI is going global (worth mentioning to sound current)
NPCI International (NIPL) is actively exporting it:

Already live / integrating: UAE, Singapore (PayNow↔UPI linkage), Sri Lanka, Nepal, Bhutan, Mauritius, France (partially, via Lyra).
Strategy: enable Indian travelers/diaspora to pay abroad, and license the UPI tech stack to other countries to build their own versions.
So the better framing isn't "UPI failed globally" — it's "UPI is hard to replicate because it depends on India-specific conditions and threatens entrenched profit models, but it's gradually expanding via bilateral linkages."

One-line interview answer:

"UPI isn't global mainly because it depends on India-specific enablers — a single coordinating body (NPCI), Aadhaar-based digital identity, and government push — and because its zero-fee model threatens the interchange revenue that Visa, Mastercard, and banks depend on. It's technically exportable, and NPCI is now linking it with countries like Singapore and the UAE, but the economic and regulatory incentives elsewhere are the real barrier — not the technology."

